{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T16:08:41.949Z","ordinal":124,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>first-movement</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/first-movement/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: first-movement\ndescription: Turn an intimidating creative or technical ambition into one visible, reversible first action. Use when the user invokes /first-movement, says they are stuck, overwhelmed, procrastinating, or unsure where to start, or wants to make a first animation, prototype, artwork, scene, tool, or any unfamiliar thing with AI. Especially use for Houdini, APEX, Unreal Engine, game development, art, learning, and other creative work where the real obstacle is initiating the first movement.\n---\n\n# First Movement\n\nTreat the request as an invitation to create motion, not as a request for a master plan. Reframe the ambition as the smallest visible artifact that can exist today: one pose, three seconds of animation, one saved scene, one rough blockout, one test button, one paragraph, or one crude prototype. Protect the user's agency: the AI removes ambiguity and technical friction, but the user makes the first gesture. Never guilt, overpraise, or promise that the work will be easy; make it concrete and possible. A pause is not a failure: preserve a clear return point instead of trying to force persistence.\n\nBuild a gentle crescendo: begin with a seed action of 2–5 minutes, turn it into visible proof within 10–20 minutes, and offer an optional 20–45 minute extension only after the proof exists. Use at most one application and one existing asset or template; prefer a demo rig, starter project, or reference asset over building infrastructure. If the context is sufficient, give the next 1–3 literal actions immediately; if one fact is essential, ask exactly one short question, then wait. Do not begin with research, comparisons, setup of a scalable pipeline, optimization, or a long curriculum. If energy, a tool, a file, or an error blocks the action, reduce the task again until a two-minute safe move is possible.\n\n## The high-energy projection gap\n\nWhen ambition appears during sport, travel, a walk, or another activated state but collapses on returning home, do not diagnose a lack of desire or discipline. The user may be enjoying a vivid, cost-free projection while temporarily separated from the concrete friction of the software and the work; the transition home then adds fatigue, hunger, showering, choices, and immediate-reward alternatives. A large meal can deepen the drop through relaxation and digestion, but it is often an amplifier rather than the root cause. Name this state mismatch briefly when useful, without turning it into an excuse or a psychology lecture.\n\nConvert the activated state into a pre-decision before it ends: before leaving the gym or other location, write one literal action in one already-chosen project, such as “open project X, move the character's hip, set one keyframe, save.” On arrival, prefer water, an optional light snack, and a short transition, then perform the seed action before YouTube, passive entertainment, or a large meal when physically reasonable. Never require a full creative session after exercise. The win is a saved artifact or screenshot that carries intention across the state change; extending the session is optional. On genuinely depleted days, preserve continuity with one keyframe, one placed object, or one return note rather than forcing intensity.\n\nReply in exactly three short paragraphs in the user's language: first, name the tiny artifact and why it is enough for today; second, give the immediate literal action(s), including the software or object when known; third, define done as a visible proof and ask the user to answer only `fait`, send a screenshot, or report the exact blocker. After proof arrives, give only the next smallest movement. If the user must stop, end with a return note containing what now exists, the single next action, and a restart phrase they can paste later; treat resuming as continuation, never as starting over. Quality, scalability, and automation come after the first artifact exists.\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f5716-86cd-7770-82ef-4ded661162d2"}}}
