{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T21:20:29.787Z","ordinal":313,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>first-movement</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/first-movement/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: first-movement\ndescription: Turn an intimidating creative or technical ambition into one visible, reversible first action. Use when the user invokes /first-movement, says they are stuck, overwhelmed, procrastinating, or unsure where to start, or wants to make a first animation, prototype, artwork, scene, tool, or any unfamiliar thing with AI. Especially use for Houdini, APEX, Unreal Engine, game development, art, learning, and other creative work where the real obstacle is initiating the first movement.\n---\n\n# First Movement\n\nTreat the request as an invitation to create motion, not as a request for a master plan. Reframe the ambition as the smallest visible artifact that can exist today: one pose, three seconds of animation, one saved scene, one rough blockout, one test button, one paragraph, or one crude prototype. Protect the user's agency: the AI removes ambiguity and technical friction, but the user makes the first gesture. Never guilt, overpraise, or promise that the work will be easy; make it concrete and possible. A pause is not a failure: preserve a clear return point instead of trying to force persistence.\n\nBuild a gentle crescendo: begin with a seed action of 2–5 minutes, turn it into visible proof within 10–20 minutes, and offer an optional 20–45 minute extension only after the proof exists. Use at most one application and one existing asset or template; prefer a demo rig, starter project, or reference asset over building infrastructure. If the context is sufficient, give the next 1–3 literal actions immediately; if one fact is essential, ask exactly one short question, then wait. Do not begin with research, comparisons, setup of a scalable pipeline, optimization, or a long curriculum. If energy, a tool, a file, or an error blocks the action, reduce the task again until a two-minute safe move is possible.\n\n## Make the movement executable\n\nDo not mistake a prerequisite for the first artifact. “Install the software,” “open the project,” “choose an animation,” or “watch this tutorial” only moves the ambiguity forward. Installation may be the first click, but the response must continue through the exact creative operation that follows it. Decide the application, existing asset or template, concrete content, output name, stopping point, and visible proof for the user. Verify current paths, labels, and shortcuts against primary documentation when a changing interface matters.\n\nApply the tired-user test: the user should be able to execute without choosing the software, project, asset, subject, movement, duration, or meaning of “done.” Make those small reversible decisions on their behalf unless they would change the larger creative direction. For character animation, specify the character or rig, one named movement, its duration or frame range, the key poses with timings, the project or sequence filename, and the capture to produce. Prefer a bounded technical spike inside the user's intended production pipeline over an isolated preview that teaches no transferable gesture. The user must manipulate at least one meaningful control; merely watching a stock animation is consumption, not a first movement.\n\n## The high-energy projection gap\n\nWhen ambition appears during sport, travel, a walk, or another activated state but collapses on returning home, do not diagnose a lack of desire or discipline. The user may be enjoying a vivid, cost-free projection while temporarily separated from the concrete friction of the software and the work; the transition home then adds fatigue, hunger, showering, choices, and immediate-reward alternatives. A large meal can deepen the drop through relaxation and digestion, but it is often an amplifier rather than the root cause. Name this state mismatch briefly when useful, without turning it into an excuse or a psychology lecture.\n\nConvert the activated state into a pre-decision before it ends: before leaving the gym or other location, write one literal action in one already-chosen project, such as “open project X, move the character's hip, set one keyframe, save.” On arrival, prefer water, an optional light snack, and a short transition, then perform the seed action before YouTube, passive entertainment, or a large meal when physically reasonable. Never require a full creative session after exercise. The win is a saved artifact or screenshot that carries intention across the state change; extending the session is optional. On genuinely depleted days, preserve continuity with one keyframe, one placed object, or one return note rather than forcing intensity.\n\nReply in exactly three short paragraphs in the user's language: first, name the tiny artifact and why it is enough for today; second, give the immediate literal action(s), including the software or object when known; third, define done as a visible proof and ask the user to answer only `fait`, send a screenshot, or report the exact blocker. After proof arrives, give only the next smallest movement. If the user must stop, end with a return note containing what now exists, the single next action, and a restart phrase they can paste later; treat resuming as continuation, never as starting over. Quality, scalability, and automation come after the first artifact exists.\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f5833-fc1a-70f1-8417-0149216d78e2"}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-07-12T21:20:29.787Z","ordinal":314,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"message","role":"user","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"<skill>\n<name>think-of-everything</name>\n<path>/Users/zenray/.codex/skills/think-of-everything/SKILL.md</path>\n---\nname: think-of-everything\ndescription: Run a systematic impact and premortem pass on any decision or change in any domain — code, architecture, product, business, life/admin, travel, purchases, health, learning, art/3D/creative. Reads the request four ways (literal, inverse, adjacent, reframed), verifies the real facts instead of guessing from memory (grep the codebase, read the actual documents and numbers, search official sources), checks a 12-axis impact surface (existing state, conflicts, limits and deadlines, security/legal, partial failure, backward compatibility, scale, recurring cost, observability, edge cases, checks, reversibility), runs a domain-specific premortem, then delivers at most 5 ranked risks with exact proof and a concrete verification each, ending in a GO / NO-GO verdict at paid-specialist precision, never generic. Use whenever the user says \"pense à tout\", \"think of everything\", \"qu'est-ce que j'ai oublié\", \"what am I missing\", asks what a change or decision might break, or wants a premortem, dependency check, or impact analysis. Also trigger proactively, without the phrase, before shipping anything touching shared state, storage or quotas, concurrency, auth or permissions, payments, a public API/route — or any decision that is expensive, irreversible, or deadline-bound.\n---\n\n# Think of Everything (Pense à Tout)\n\nSurface the FEW risks that matter on ANY decision — code, business, life, art — each proven against the real facts, each with a concrete check, ending in a decision. 40 generic checklist items is a failure; 5 proven, ranked risks is a success. This pass answers the question asked, its opposite, and the ones nobody asked, then verifies against reality instead of memory.\n\n**Specialist rule (anti-generic), applies to every output line:** write at the level of the expert who gets paid to review exactly this — immigration lawyer, senior rigger, tax advisor, DB architect. Every kept risk must contain at least one exact value from the real context (number, date, name, path, price, spec) verified this session. A risk that could be pasted unchanged into someone else's situation is generic: sharpen it or cut it.\n\n## Step 0: Classify and scale (two lines)\n\n- Domain + the specialist to embody + type: (a) action to take, (b) existing thing to audit, (c) pure decision, nothing built yet.\n- For (b), the source of truth is the actual artifact (`git diff`, the contract, the scene file), never its description.\n- Trivial, cheap and reversible case: say so, jump to Step 4 + verdict, 5 output lines max. The full pass is for anything expensive, irreversible, deadline-bound, or touching shared state, money, auth, public APIs.\n\n## Step 1: Read the request four ways (one line each)\n\n1. **Literal**: restate exactly what was asked.\n2. **Inverse**: what if the premise is false? What single condition breaks this? And what does NOT doing it cost?\n3. **Adjacent**: the 2-3 questions the specialist would ask next, unprompted. Parallel questions, not follow-ups.\n4. **Reframed**: the clearest version of this request. If it differs from the literal one, answer that too.\n\n## Step 2: Verify the real facts (never from memory)\n\n**Evidence rule, the core of this skill: every claim cites its proof — file:line, URL of an official source, a measured value, or a posed calculation — found THIS SESSION, or carries the label [non vérifié]. No exceptions.**\n\nSources by domain:\n\n- **Code**: `rg -n \"<symbol|route|table|key>\"` across the whole repo — every call site, not just the edited file. Hyrum's law: who depends on the CURRENT undocumented behavior (output format, ordering, timing, error text)? Does the new code re-read data written by the old code?\n- **Business / life / admin**: read the actual documents (contracts, statements, notes, Memory files); web-search official sources for external facts (rules, prices, deadlines, opening days); compute the real numbers and dates — count, never estimate.\n- **Physical / creative (3D, drawing, video, print)**: open the actual source files (scene, layers, footage); check the official specs (software docs, platform limits, physical medium constraints); measure, don't eyeball.\n- **People**: who else is affected, who must agree, whose workflow breaks if this changes?\n\nIf the facts can't be reached, label the whole map \"non vérifié / spéculatif\". Never present a guess as a finding.\n\n## Step 3: Impact surface (12 universal axes, one killer question each)\n\n1. **Existing state**: does what already exists (data, commitments, acquired rights) stay valid? Partial overwrite possible?\n2. **Conflicts / concurrency**: two actors or processes on the same resource at once — then what?\n3. **Limits & deadlines**: which cap (budget, size, rate, date, capacity) gets hit first, and what happens at the boundary?\n4. **Security / legal**: who can trigger or abuse this? Compliant with law, terms, licenses?\n5. **Partial failure**: stopped halfway — what state remains, is it recoverable?\n6. **Backward compatibility**: old data, clients, habits, commitments still work?\n7. **Scale & durability**: still fine at 100x the volume, or repeated weekly for a year?\n8. **Recurring cost**: money, time, energy that scales with usage?\n9. **Observability**: if it fails silently, how and when do you find out?\n10. **Edge cases**: empty/first-run, oversized, duplicate, offline; holiday, sick day, exception case.\n11. **Checks**: a test or verification that catches the regression BEFORE real impact?\n12. **Reversibility**: one action to undo? Does what got engaged meanwhile (money sent, word given, data written) survive?\n\nKeep only the applicable axes, expressed in the domain's own units and vocabulary. Dismiss the rest in one line (\"N/A: ...\"), never pad.\n\n## Step 4: Premortem (narrative, not checklist)\n\n\"Three weeks later it failed, and it was THIS decision. Write the most plausible incident, then the second.\" Use THIS domain's high-frequency classics — code: off-by-one, empty/first-run state, stale cache, non-idempotent retry, timezone/encoding, two concurrent writers; admin/life: missed deadline, missing document, misread eligibility rule, transfer delay, closed office; business: nobody pays, no distribution channel; 3D/art/print: wrong units or scale, topology breaks at rig, export color shift, asset license.\n\n## Step 5: Rank, cut, decide\n\n- Rank each risk: severity (cosmetic → irreversible loss) x likelihood. Irreversible bumps severity one level.\n- Keep the top 5 maximum. Dismissed risks get one summary line total.\n- Every kept risk carries its **proof** (per Step 2) and **one concrete check** to confirm or kill it: a command, a test, a call, a calculation, a measurement.\n\n## Output format (compact, French by default on this account)\n\n```\nDécision/changement : 1 à 3 lignes.\nAngles morts (4 lectures) : 1 ligne chacune.\n| # | Risque | Preuve | Sév x Prob | Vérification |\nÉcartés : 1 ligne.\nQuestions ouvertes : numérotées, chacune avec un défaut recommandé (accord par silence).\nVerdict : GO / GO avec corrections (listées) / NO-GO + correction minimale.\n```\n\n## Stop conditions and anti-goals\n\n- **ONE pass.** Re-run only if the facts changed afterwards, and only on the delta. Never apply this skill to its own output.\n- It audits ONE stated decision or change. It does not generate unsolicited master plans.\n- Not a substitute for tests, CI, or licensed professionals on high-stakes legal, medical or financial moves: it catches known failure classes, not novel ones. Say that plainly instead of implying completeness.\n\n</skill>"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"019f5833-fc1a-70f1-8417-0149216d78e2"}}}
