lick Accept.\nL24: ## Tool Settings\nL25: \nL26: You use the Tool Properties panel to control settings such as the output type, dimensions, and material.\nL27: \nL28: As with other Modeling Mode tools, parameter values are remembered when reopening the tool.\nL29: ### Output Type\nL30: \nL31: The Output Type sets the type of mesh you create. You can choose between the following types:\nL32: \nL33:   * Static mesh\nL34:   * Dynamic mesh\nL35:   * Volume\nL36: \nL37: You can update the mesh type at any stage of the modeling process by various tools, such as Convert and Transfer, found in the Transform category.\nL38: \nL39: To learn more about these output types and asset management, refer to the cite3†Working with Meshes documentation.\nL40: ### Shape\nL41: \nL42: You can adjust the dimensions and subdivisions of your mesh under the Shape setting. Each shape has specific options.\nL43: \nL44: In addition, you can configure the PolyGroups of your new mesh using the PolyGroup Mode setting. The Polygroup Mode has the following grouping options:\nL45: cite31†Image: Generate PolyGroups per Shape†dev.epicgames.com cite32†Image: Generate PolyGroups per Face†dev.epicgames.com cite33†Image: Generate PolyGroups per Quad†dev.epicgames.com L46: Per Shape Per Face Per Quad\nL47: Outputs the entire mesh as a single group. Automatically divide the mesh into recognizable face groups. Automatically divide the mesh into a group for each quad.\nL48: \nL49: To learn more about PolyGroups, refer to the cite4†Understanding PolyGroups documentation.\nL50: ### Positioning\nL51: \nL52: You can position your mesh in your level based on your scene or the ground plane.\nL53: \nL54: Choosing On Scene from Target Surface positions your mesh based on the surface normal of the geometry your cursor resides over.\nL55: \nL56: If you set cite5†Collision Presets to No Collision for any object in your level, then On Scene will not detect the object.\nL57: \nL58: Choosing Ground Plane p